// Expected: a0.
//
// `k` is a shared private that never leaks, so the attacker cannot build any
// ciphertext `AEAD_DEC(kk_b, e, nil)?` will accept.  The only `e` it can put on
// the wire is Alice's own, and forwarding a message unchanged is a relay, not a
// forgery: Bob receives exactly what Alice sent.  So the query holds.
//
// `ga` is the trap.  It is unguarded, Bob uses it, and the attacker holds other
// protocol terms of the same shape (`gb`) to put there — so a proposal that
// substitutes `ga` is a genuine attack on something.  That proposal carries a
// relay of every other controlled slot, `e` among them, because the honest
// ciphertext has to be on the wire for Bob to reach the substituted value at
// all.  If attacker authorship is decided per proposal rather than per slot,
// the relayed `e` is stamped attacker-sent and this query reports a false
// attack.
//
// This is the shape that made `examples/messaging/signal.vp` report a false
// attack on the first message: the distinguishing substitution was on the
// *third* message's ephemeral key, and `e1` merely rode along.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	generates m, a
	ga = G^a
	kk = HASH(k)
	e = AEAD_ENC(kk, m, nil)
]

principal Bob[
	knows private k, b
	gb = G^b
]

Bob -> Alice: gb

Alice -> Bob: ga, e

principal Bob[
	kk_b = HASH(k)
	m_b = AEAD_DEC(kk_b, e, nil)?
	shared = ga^b
	tag = MAC(shared, m_b)
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: e
]
